  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\n\nAs the tropical remnants move away from the area, rain chances are\ncoming to an end -- with only some chances in southern Ohio and\nnortheast Kentucky through evening. However, with quite a bit of low\nlevel moisture, fog will become likely overnight. Clearing skies from\nwest-to-east will favor fog development near and west of the\nInterstate 75 corridor, but anywhere that clears out (and especially\nlocations with light or calm winds) will have the potential for fog\ndevelopment. Not out of the question that some dense fog will be\npossible, so an advisory could be needed at some point after fog\ndevelopment begins and the locations can be determined. This will be\nmore likely for the western half of the ILN CWA, but at least some\nreduction in visibility appears likely for much of the forecast area.\n\nKey Message 2)\n\nHigh pressure will bring dry conditions to the Ohio Valley on Monday,\nand also likely on Tuesday as well -- though a weak disturbance will\nmove through the Great Lakes, it will probably stay just north of the\nILN forecast area.\n\nA more active weather pattern is expected to develop by Wednesday, as\na surface front sets up west-to-east somewhere across the region, and\nmoisture begins flowing into the Ohio Valley around a mid-level ridge\nover the southeastern states. This setup will likely provide chances\nfor rain (and storms) on occasion from early Wednesday through\nFriday, with precipitation also occurring with a cold front moving\nthrough the region some time on Friday. No obvious signs for\nhazardous weather with thunderstorms from Wednesday through Friday as\nof now.\n\nTemperatures will likely peak out on Tuesday, with highs in the mid\n80s, before gradually falling the next couple days. A more\nsignificant drop in temperatures is expected behind the cold front on\nFriday, leading to temperatures below normal over the weekend. Lows\nmay even get into the 30s on Sunday morning, so some frost potential\nmay exist.\n\nFinally, some gusty winds may be possible Friday behind the cold\nfront -- still quite a ways out in the forecast, but something to\nkeep an eye on as it gets closer.",
